WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.540
Our next speaker is one of our
keynote speakers for the day.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:20.350
And he is Brian Nosek.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.900
Brian is Co-founder
and Executive Director

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.140
of the Center for Open Science,
which operates the Open Science

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.640
Framework.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.120
The Center for Open Science
is a nonprofit technology

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.790
organization based in
Charlottesville, Virginia

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:37.300
with a mission to increase
the openness, integrity,

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.270
and reproducibility of
scientific research.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.670
Brian is a professor in the
Department of Psychology

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:44.530
at the University of Virginia.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.030
He received his
undergraduate degree

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.320
from California Polytechnic
State University and a PhD

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.840
from Yale University.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.140
Welcome home.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.260
He co-founded Project
Implicit, an internet-based

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.800
multi-university collaboration
between researchers

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.580
and education investigating
implicit cognition.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.860
Important topic today.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.080
Professor Nosek
investigates the gap

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.350
between values and practices,
such as when behavior

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:15.590
is influenced by factors
other than one's intentions

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:16.460
and goals.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.370
Research applications
of this interest

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:23.570
are implicit bias, decision
making, attitudes, ideology,

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:26.430
morality, and
barriers to change.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.130
He applies the
scientific research

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.070
to improve the alignment between
personal and organizational

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.290
values and practices.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.930
In 2015 Professor
Nosek was named

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.840
as one of Nature
magazine's Nature's 10.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.710
And in the same year, The
Chronicle of Higher Education

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.110
added him to its Influence List.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.030
Welcome, Professor Brian Nosek.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:51.840
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:52.580
Thanks very much.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.560
You want to get me started here?

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.040
I very much appreciate the
invitation for this event

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.420
and hope that what
I talk about is

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:03.920
seen as very relevant
to the things

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.270
that the institution and the
university is working on.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.880
I'm Nosek there.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.710
Thank you.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:13.340
So the Center for Open
Science was founded in 2013.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.640
It is a nonprofit
technology company,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.980
as Jill mentioned,
with a mission

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.620
to increase openness,
integrity, and reproducibility

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:22.850
of research.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:25.490
And we have four primary
activities-- thank you--

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:26.330
for that.

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:28.670
One is meta science,
where we are conducting

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.940
large-scale studies
of the reproducibility

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.540
of different fields, or research
on just scientific practices

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.320
itself to identify areas
to improve reproducibility.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.350
A second is that we
conduct trainings

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.930
about how to be more
reproducible or open in one's

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:47.000
research, what
tools are available,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.760
and how to use them effectively.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.860
A third area that gets
a lot of our attention

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:55.370
is working on the
incentives in scholarship

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.280
to try to align
what's good for me

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.980
as a scientist with
what's good for science.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.410
So can we make it
in one's interest

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.570
to be more open and
reproducible in their work,

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.190
make that part of
the reward structure

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:10.280
rather than having it just
focused on publication

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.770
as frequently as possible in
the most prestigious outlets

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:13.620
possible?

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.120
That was the focus of my
presentation yesterday.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.370
What I want to focus on today
is the last thing that we do,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:21.950
which is most of the staff.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.190
We have about 75 people now at
the center, and most of them

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.580
are working on
infrastructure, building tools

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:33.440
to help open and enable greater
integration and reproducibility

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.890
of the entire research
lifecycle as public goods

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:40.580
infrastructure for
the community to use.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:44.000
So let me open with sort of
what the organizing principle

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.560
of our strategy for
research infrastructure.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.810
And that is that academic
scholarship, when

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.850
done for academic
purposes, is a public good,

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.805
that what the pursuit
of knowledge is

00:03:55.805 --> 00:03:57.650
is not for my
knowledge unless I'm

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.650
pursuing competitive advantage
or commercialization.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.870
But when I'm pursuing it
for academic purposes,

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:05.780
I'm pursuing it for
the good of humanity

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.520
to know new things that I have
contributed as a researcher.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.720
So if we think of the role of
scholarship as a public good,

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.910
a concrete application
of that is,

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:20.329
what does it mean to have
that be a public good?

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:23.110
And there are three areas
that we pay most attention to

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:24.320
in openness.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.440
One is open access of the
outcomes-- the publications

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.500
that result, the scholarship
that comes out of what it is I

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.750
found in my process
of discovery,

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:36.480
of my generation of data and
interpretation of that data.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.110
Second is the theme of
the day, the open data,

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.960
the content of research, and
not just the data in terms

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.110
of the units that were
generated from the methodology,

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:47.637
but information about
the methodology itself,

00:04:47.637 --> 00:04:49.970
the materials that were done,
the process that was done,

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:51.440
the code that was generated.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.030
All of that is the
content of what's

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:54.500
actually in the research.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.280
As David Donahoe
at Stanford said,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.870
the publication is really
advertising for the research.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:02.540
The research itself is down
here, the content of what

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:06.080
was generated that led
to that publication.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.420
And the third area
we think is vital,

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.640
and not as much part of
the conversation yet,

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.460
but really is the critical part
of representing that research

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.610
lifecycle, which is the
workflow, the process

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:19.340
of generating that data,
of drawing inference,

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:20.840
of coming up with designs.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.870
How is it that I conducted
that research in order

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:26.100
to arrive at those outcomes?

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.150
And that has just as much
a role to play as the data

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:31.180
itself that was generated.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:34.370
And so that really is a key
part of our overall strategy

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.670
of trying to open up the
entire research lifecycle.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.040
So let me just drill down
a bit into the benefits

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.890
that we perceive of open
workflows to then transition

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.890
into our technology strategy.

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:46.682
One is obvious.

00:05:46.682 --> 00:05:47.390
It's in the name.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.440
It increases process
transparency.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.320
How is it that I
arrived at my research?

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:52.820
If you can see my
workflow, if you

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:56.090
can see the history
of my work, then you

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:58.700
have information about
that and can evaluate it.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:02.630
It increases my accountability
to how it is I draw inference.

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:05.096
If I do 500 different tests
on a particular data set

00:06:05.096 --> 00:06:07.220
and only share five of them
with you, the ones that

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:10.379
looked really good, if you can
see my process of doing that,

00:06:10.379 --> 00:06:12.920
then you can decide whether that
was a reasonable thing to do

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:13.520
or not.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.010
If you don't see my process,
then you don't know,

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.470
and I have no accountability
to other observers

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:21.540
to how it is I
generated my findings.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.270
Open workflows also
facilitate reproducibility

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.760
in that if you see how it
is I conducted the research,

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.040
you have much more
information to be able to do

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.800
that same thing yourself.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.020
Also, it facilitates
metascience.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.690
If you want to study
how it is people draw

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.210
scientific inferences, or
scholarly inferences more

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.310
generally, you
need to have access

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.860
to how it is that those
inferences are generated.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.320
And so conducting research
on the research process

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.320
itself requires access
to the data about how

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.600
the research was conducted.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.580
Moreover, it fosters
collaboration

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:55.890
in that as those
workflows are open,

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.140
there are more opportunities
for people with expertise

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.681
at different stages
of the life cycle

00:06:59.681 --> 00:07:02.760
to get involved in
that research process.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.840
Researchers in laboratories
around our university--

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.250
perhaps here as well--

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:13.020
don't think a lot about curation
of their data in advance.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.530
But those are skills that
are available on university

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:17.070
campuses.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.087
Librarians have lots
of duration skills

00:07:19.087 --> 00:07:21.420
that, if they could get
inserted earlier in the research

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.420
process, could help
set up data so that it

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:27.000
is more accessible, more
reusable, and better

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:30.120
curated for future use.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.550
Also, open workflows
foster inclusivity

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.894
by giving more touch
points for people

00:07:34.894 --> 00:07:36.810
with different areas of
expertise and interest

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.540
to get involved in the
research lifecycle, wherever it

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:42.840
is they could be contributors.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:44.460
Moreover, that
fosters innovation,

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:47.310
because as you can see
more of the lifecycle,

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.860
there are more opportunities
to identify friction points

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:54.000
or gaps that could be addressed
with different types of tools.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.820
And most critically
for our purposes

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:00.540
is that an open workflow
protects against lock-in.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:06.390
So an easy strategy to build
commercial business on research

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:08.460
is to get researchers
in at one point

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:12.240
and keep them in the types
of tools that one develops.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.320
Apple has a fantastic strategy
to keep me using Apple products

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.630
by connecting all those
things together so that I'm

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:19.650
dependent on them.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:22.350
And I thank them
for it, and I keep

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:24.200
buying more things from them.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:27.450
So it is a great strategy
for commercialization,

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.690
but it isn't a great strategy
for research advancement

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:33.840
for public goods
because if I am locked

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.940
in a particular set of
tools and services, then

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.485
I don't have
opportunity to innovate.

00:08:37.485 --> 00:08:38.860
I don't have
opportunity to bring

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:41.159
in other services that might
be most relevant to what

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:42.720
it is I need to do.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:46.320
And it's then contingent on a
particular provider rather than

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.810
all of the potential service
providers of a research

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.090
ecosystem.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:54.360
So that sort of is the baseline
core of what the Open Science

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.400
Framework aims to do.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.000
And really, the
representation, the process

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.540
that we are trying to have an
impact on in reproducibility

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.490
is that entire
research lifecycle,

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:08.310
from the generation of ideas
to the collection of that data

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.860
to how it is stored and
analyzed, how it is then

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:12.780
represented and interpreted.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:15.630
And so all of the tools
are reinforcing that.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:17.130
So I want to give
a quick impression

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.260
of what the overall strategy
is and then unpack it

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:21.120
piece by piece.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.230
We essentially are pursuing
three layers of infrastructure

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:28.800
to help support opening up
the entire research lifecycle.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.610
A base level infrastructure
of the tools and services

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:36.570
that developers create
and maintain that

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:39.420
then facilitate the
creation of interfaces,

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.880
ways in which researchers
then engage with those tools

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:44.430
and services in order to
conduct their research

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.590
at different parts
of the lifecycle.

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:50.370
And then those interfaces
enable branding and engagement

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:53.340
of communities by them
taking those services

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:55.672
and having it represented
for their own community

00:09:55.672 --> 00:09:57.630
with their own norms,
with their own standards,

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.250
with their own
rules of how it is

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:03.406
their community engages in that
type or that area of research.

00:10:03.406 --> 00:10:05.280
And so what I'll do for
the rest of the talk,

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:06.930
essentially, is
unpack each of these

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.360
and show how it is
they fit together,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.470
starting at the bottom, so the
core set of tools and services

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:16.200
that the team is developing.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.630
So the first one
is the tool kit.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:20.694
So most of the team, over
the last three and 1/2

00:10:20.694 --> 00:10:22.110
years that we've
been working, has

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:26.190
been developing a
suite of open services

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.250
that are composable,
that represent workflow,

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:32.160
authentication, how one
gets access to a system,

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.200
permissions, what
different users have access

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:37.650
to, file storage
tools, file rendering

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:41.130
in a browser, persistence
and a meta-database,

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:43.710
integrations connecting
services together,

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:45.750
search and discovery
functionality,

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.940
and the shared data set.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:50.670
These are composable
tools so that anyone that

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:54.450
is trying to build a new service
that operates via the internet

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:57.030
can draw on any of
these pieces in order

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:59.820
to lower the barrier to
entry, make it easier and more

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:02.730
efficient for them to create a
new service, a new interface,

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:05.940
a new way to engage researchers,
but on a common framework

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:09.630
of tools so that it is much
more efficient and much more

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:12.630
interoperable across the
different tools and services

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:14.160
that researchers create.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:15.900
There's a whole lot that we
could talk about with that,

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:17.483
but I just want to
give you impression

00:11:17.483 --> 00:11:18.510
of what the toolkit is.

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:22.740
And you want all of it,
it's all right there.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:25.320
So that is critical
because this is

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:29.050
the basis of all of the things
that get built on top of it.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:33.150
And one of the key pieces
is the services ecosystem.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:35.490
What the services
ecosystem is is it's

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:39.150
based on an abstracted API
at the core of these tools

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.500
and services.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.960
And the entire idea
of this approach

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:46.680
is to have any service that is
connected to the Open Science

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.320
Framework is then connected
to all of the other services

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:51.390
around the research lifecycle.

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:55.050
So for example, right now we
have connections with Mendeley

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.080
and Zotero as citation
management tools,

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:00.810
some data collection
tools like Open Sesame,

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:03.570
data storage environments like
Google Drive or Amazon Web

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:06.900
Services, Dropbox, GitHub,
statistical analytics,

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:10.530
tools like JASP Stats-- it's
an open source analytics tool--

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:13.200
and then places where
people archive their data

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:15.640
like FigShare and Dataverse

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:20.430
So as each service adds their
connection to the Open Science

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:23.100
Framework, then
they can speak with,

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:26.670
interact with all of the other
services in that ecosystem.

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:30.120
And once that happens,
then lock-in is prevented.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:32.970
If I have my data in Dataverse
and then suddenly Dataverse

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:34.470
goes commercial and
bananas and they

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.120
start to do things
that I don't like,

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:39.390
I can move my
content out of that

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:42.000
instantly into another service.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.760
And I can add additional
services to my workflow

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:48.269
by just connecting
different tools that I use.

00:12:48.269 --> 00:12:50.310
So those are the ones that
are already completed.

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:53.640
We gave out grants to a
variety of different groups

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:57.240
to add additional services to
just demonstrate connections

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.820
across the entire
research lifecycle,

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:02.520
so that, for example,
you can imagine someone

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:05.430
doing their data management
planning with DMP tool.

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:07.480
And instead of that
being a static document

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:09.960
that, once I get the
grant, I never look

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.940
at again, because why
would I look at it again?

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:12.606
I got the grant.

00:13:12.606 --> 00:13:14.470
I'm just doing my research now.

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:18.000
Instead, that can become
a living document that

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:20.160
travels along the
research lifecycle,

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:23.310
and as the relevant
items in the data plan

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.740
become relevant in
the research process,

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:27.960
then we might be able to
facilitate and connect

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.360
that with the
movement of that data

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:34.040
into data storage
devices, et cetera.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:37.700
So just to give one
illustration of this,

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:41.090
Notre Dame has a computational
services environment

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:43.340
for their researchers,
and they were

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:45.410
building an interface
to try to connect that

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:48.480
to different data storage
environments, box.com, Dropbox,

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:49.820
et cetera.

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:53.390
They discovered that we started
to collaborate and identify

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:56.120
areas of common
interest, and instead

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:58.580
of continuing on developing
their own interface,

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:01.520
they connected their
computational services platform

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:03.080
to the OSF.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:05.300
And then because it's
connected to this API,

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:08.450
now instantly their
computational services

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:10.430
is connected to all of
the data stores that

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:11.750
are connected to the OSF.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:15.050
So they could say, call from
this data storage environment,

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:16.640
send it to our
computational platform,

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:20.500
and then output the results
to the Open Science Framework.

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:22.930
This has a dramatic
effect on efficiency

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:26.620
because every time someone
else adds their data storage

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:29.320
environment to the OSF,
then that's instantly

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:32.740
part of their computational
services platform.

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:35.801
And they only have to maintain
one relationship with the Open

00:14:35.801 --> 00:14:36.550
Science Framework.

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:38.320
They don't have to worry
about all of the connections

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:39.736
with all the other
services, which

00:14:39.736 --> 00:14:42.190
are continually
getting updated and is

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:44.830
a substantial technical burden.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.410
So the efficiency gains
for each organization,

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:50.440
each group involved, having
a single point of contact

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:53.140
can dramatically accelerate
the connection of services

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:54.875
more broadly.

00:14:54.875 --> 00:14:57.250
We can talk about that more
if there are questions later,

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:59.333
but I want to move on to
some of the other pieces,

00:14:59.333 --> 00:15:02.710
because that's really at the
core of a lot of what we do.

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:05.380
The next one is
data, and I'll just

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:09.520
talk first about metadata
of scholarly events.

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:12.770
And this is primarily through
a project called Share,

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:15.130
and this is a collaboration
with the Association

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:17.050
of Research Libraries.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:20.980
And the idea of
Share is to identify

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:24.250
all of the different
providers of scholarly events.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:26.680
So that might be a publication,
it might be a grant award,

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:29.110
might be a clinical
trial registration,

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:32.360
it might be an individual
researcher doing something,

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:35.680
it might be a data set being
made available in a repository.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:39.130
There are lots of
different services

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:42.940
that have these
events occur and have

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.430
some kind of alert or
access point of metadata

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:47.350
about what that event is.

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:49.210
But they're all in
their own formats.

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:51.310
They all have different
metadata styles.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:54.330
And so what Share
does is it takes

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:58.630
all of those different
services and normalizes them

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:00.730
into a single
format so that there

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:05.320
can be one pipeline of all
research events of any kind

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:08.770
where you can search against
that particular for anything

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:11.230
from any source.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:14.440
Once we have that
pipeline, then we

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:17.330
are building curation
tools on top of it.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:21.130
And so part of the key
with collaboration with ARL

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:23.410
is that there are
curation associates that

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:25.780
will connect these
different research events,

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:28.270
so that that data
set, oh, that's

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:31.780
related to that publication,
oh, and that grant award.

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:35.380
And if we can just connect them
together as related events,

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:36.880
then it's a lot
easier to discover.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:38.740
For example, if
institutions want

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.050
to know what their
researchers are doing,

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:45.400
then a curated data set
can allow the development

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:47.380
of discovery layers
on top of that

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:50.980
that are focused on research
events by their faculty.

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:54.610
So there are many different
uses for an open dataset

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:56.500
of all research
events because you

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:58.720
can develop lots of
different discovery layers

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:00.250
on top of that.

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:01.960
So where we are
so far with Share

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:05.829
is that there are 16
million events, as of a day

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:08.260
or two ago when I
screenshot this,

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:11.050
and from 126 different sources.

00:17:11.050 --> 00:17:13.810
That's institutional
repositories

00:17:13.810 --> 00:17:17.380
with groups like CrossRef
or Datasite or FigShare.

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:20.589
And they are all searchable
through a single interface.

00:17:20.589 --> 00:17:24.190
And that shared dataset, now,
because its an open data set,

00:17:24.190 --> 00:17:26.849
can be applied to any new
service that anybody builds.

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:30.160
They can use the shared data
set as their search engine,

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:34.940
and that's what we use for all
the services that we develop.

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:38.510
So that's sort of the core
layer of tools and services

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:41.810
that other things that
researchers need to use

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:43.094
can be built upon.

00:17:43.094 --> 00:17:45.260
So I want to talk next about
the second layer, which

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:48.170
is the interfaces that
we're developing as example

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.930
cases for what
researchers can build,

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:52.700
or what scholarly
communities can

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:57.680
build to interact with the types
of scholarship that they do.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:17:59.870
Our best-known
interface, and the one

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:02.900
we've worked on the
most, is at osf.io.

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:05.660
It's also termed the
Open Science Framework,

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:08.120
but really it is
an interface, too,

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:12.230
that is separate from those
core tools and services.

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:15.500
And so what OSF does
for researchers is it's

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:17.120
a project management
environment.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:19.430
And it tries to draw on
all of those services

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:22.910
into a very flexible integrated
system that helps researchers

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:26.900
maintain their own research for
their own purposes privately,

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:28.610
but make it very
easy to make parts

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.020
of that publicly accessible.

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:34.220
So for example, you sign up for
an account as an individual.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:35.720
When you have an
account, you go in

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:36.928
and you have a project space.

00:18:36.928 --> 00:18:38.480
You can create projects.

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.760
You add collaborators
to those projects.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:43.340
You can drop data and
materials, anything digital,

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:45.470
into those projects.

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:47.180
You organize them,
and you have things

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:49.490
like Wikis and other
management things

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:52.160
just to maintain that
project internally

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:55.970
for your team's own use, a
cloud-based archiving access

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:58.850
point so that distributed
teams, collaborative teams all

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:00.260
know where to find the stuff.

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:04.370
And it will never get lost, even
if the graduate student leaves

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:06.085
or the computer explodes.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:11.462
So it sort of looks like that.

00:19:11.462 --> 00:19:14.950
A second feature of it is it
has a simplified version control

00:19:14.950 --> 00:19:15.850
interface.

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:19.300
Some researchers understand
and can use things like Git

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:22.360
to engage version control
with the work that they do,

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:25.450
but that's not something
that many researchers use,

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:29.050
even though they can appreciate
the value of version control.

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:31.900
Most of what version control
happens in-- for example,

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:33.070
authoring--

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:35.590
is creating a single
folder on one's desktop

00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:39.700
and saying, Manuscript
Final and then

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:41.890
Manuscript Final
Final, Manuscript,

00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:42.970
this is the final one.

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:44.590
Don't look at the other ones.

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:46.180
And so there's a
continuing array

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:49.750
of these versioned
articles of what

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:51.070
it was that was being written.

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:53.170
But it's very hard to
keep track of that.

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:56.230
This does it automatically so
that every time the file gets

00:19:56.230 --> 00:19:59.080
updated, then it
creates a new version,

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:01.030
but you have access
to the entire version

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:06.659
history of that file,
whatever's in the file.

00:20:06.659 --> 00:20:08.950
So it makes it much easier,
lowers the barrier to entry

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:13.660
to use things like version
control for researchers,

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:17.980
regardless of their
technology tendencies.

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:20.170
A key part of the
OSF is that it merges

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:22.060
public and private workflows.

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:23.920
One of the big
barriers to getting

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:26.920
into being more open with
one's data and research

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:30.370
is that most of the practices
now require me to add on,

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:33.460
append new work to the work
that I've already done.

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:35.380
So once I get my
publication, the journal

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:37.884
says, now would be great
if you opened your data.

00:20:37.884 --> 00:20:39.550
And I think, what are
you talking about?

00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:39.910
I'm done.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:40.959
I've got my publication.

00:20:40.959 --> 00:20:42.250
You want to do more things now?

00:20:42.250 --> 00:20:42.880
I'm busy.

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:45.010
I've got to go onto
the next publication.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:48.940
If instead we can integrate
the preparation for open data

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:51.400
with the workflow as
it happens, then we

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:53.380
don't have to append
onto the process

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:55.300
making one's data available.

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:58.540
Instead, we can
have an environment

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:00.970
that I'm using for
private purposes,

00:21:00.970 --> 00:21:03.640
but all of the components have
a little button up at the top

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:04.882
that says Make Public.

00:21:04.882 --> 00:21:06.340
And if I click the
button, it says,

00:21:06.340 --> 00:21:08.381
are you really sure you
want to make this public?

00:21:08.381 --> 00:21:11.930
And if I say yes, then that
part of the project is public.

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:14.680
And so I can continue to work
on my project while making

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:17.950
different parts of it-- whatever
parts I'm prepared to or can

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:19.330
make available--

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:20.950
make it publicly accessible.

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:23.680
And so that private
workflow for lab management

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:26.500
is simultaneously integrated
with a public workflow,

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:30.790
and there's no extra work to
make things publicly available.

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:33.346
It also integrates things
like incentives for openness.

00:21:33.346 --> 00:21:34.720
Why is it that I
want to be open?

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:36.700
Well, the things that
I can appeal to myself

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:38.920
for wanting to be open
is seeing how many

00:21:38.920 --> 00:21:41.770
people download
the work that I do,

00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:45.400
having the opportunity for
people to fork my project

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:47.650
and make use of it themselves.

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:52.780
Forking is a concept imported
from open source software

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:56.200
development that is a
functional citation.

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:58.780
You did something that
I can adapt and use,

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:01.030
so I will fork
what you have done.

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:03.130
I will make some
adaptation of it

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:05.170
for some new use,
some new design

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:07.450
of this particular study,
some new methodology that's

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:10.120
inspired by yours or
developed on top of yours.

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:13.680
But there's always a functional
link back to the origin.

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:16.000
And so as different
methodologies

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:17.890
or different
datasets evolve over

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:19.750
time with many
different contributors,

00:22:19.750 --> 00:22:21.700
you can trace the history
through the network

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:26.020
of how it is that work was
done to see an originator's

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:28.600
influence on the
development trajectory

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:32.500
or particular type of
research or phenomenon.

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:35.710
It also has persistence
citable identifiers

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:38.740
that link to the content
of the identifier

00:22:38.740 --> 00:22:43.510
rather than just being a link
to where content might persist.

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:47.930
So every piece of every
component, every project,

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:51.850
every comment, everything
that's done on the Open Science

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:54.280
Framework has a
unique identifier

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:55.510
to preserve that content.

00:22:55.510 --> 00:22:58.270
And citations are
automatically generated

00:22:58.270 --> 00:23:01.540
to facilitate the idea that
making more of one's research

00:23:01.540 --> 00:23:04.120
process available-- not just
the publication, but also

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.550
the dataset, materials,
and otherwise-- those

00:23:06.550 --> 00:23:08.620
are scholarly objects
themselves that

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:10.720
are deserving of
citation and credit

00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:14.130
as contributions, just
as the publication is.

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:19.240
Also, you can add DOIs
and other, more standard

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:22.840
persistent identifiers.

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:24.870
So I'll do two more
features and then move on

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:26.200
to the other parts.

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:28.600
And one is registration.

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:32.200
So there are different points
in the research lifecycle

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:34.450
that are important that
I want to call out,

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:38.500
as this is a point in time
that is worth paying attention

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:40.920
to and preserving.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:42.940
So with the Open
Science Framework,

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:45.700
if I am, for example,
at the point of getting

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:48.010
ready to start data
collection, and I want to say,

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:49.600
this was the state
of the project

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:50.917
as data collection commenced.

00:23:50.917 --> 00:23:52.750
Here's the design,
here's our analysis plan,

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:54.999
here's all the things that
we think we're going to do,

00:23:54.999 --> 00:23:56.440
data collection
is about to start.

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:59.740
I can register the project,
sort of provide some metadata

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:02.380
about what the project is
for, why we're doing it,

00:24:02.380 --> 00:24:04.780
what the plan is,
and then it creates

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:08.830
a permanent archived version
of the state of the project

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:10.152
at that point in time.

00:24:10.152 --> 00:24:12.610
So I can continue to work on
the project and change things,

00:24:12.610 --> 00:24:15.280
but I have a registered
version that's archived and put

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:18.310
into our long
preservations store

00:24:18.310 --> 00:24:21.160
so that none of that
information for that time

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:22.030
will ever be lost.

00:24:22.030 --> 00:24:24.880
And it's date and time
stamped, as registries do,

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:27.370
to sort of provide some
independent assurance

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:31.330
of when and what was happening.

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:34.570
Last is drawing on that
services ecosystem.

00:24:34.570 --> 00:24:36.310
It connects all those
services together,

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:38.740
but it provides an
interface so that if I

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:43.720
am using GitHub for my
code, Amazon simple storage

00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:48.142
for some large files, and
Dropbox for my everyday files,

00:24:48.142 --> 00:24:49.600
I can connect all
of those together

00:24:49.600 --> 00:24:51.490
into a single
interface and just drag

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:53.240
and drop and interact
between them,

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.840
rather than having to jump
to lots of different services

00:24:55.840 --> 00:24:59.800
and potentially lose information
or memory about where things

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:03.520
are and how to access things if
different users are using each

00:25:03.520 --> 00:25:06.420
of those different services.

00:25:06.420 --> 00:25:10.080
So that's osf.io, but that's
just one interface that

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:13.154
can be built on that
core set of services.

00:25:13.154 --> 00:25:14.820
We also have a variety
of other services

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:19.050
that we are building as
examples of interfaces that

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:22.560
can be built. But because
this is all open source,

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:25.620
the community can decide, I have
an interface, I have an idea,

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:28.770
I have a different way in which
I would draw on those services

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:31.770
to represent the scholarship
or the research process

00:25:31.770 --> 00:25:34.020
that is of interest to me.

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:36.010
So I want to give just
a couple of examples.

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:38.400
One, OSF Institutions.

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:40.800
So we have an
institutional view where,

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:44.610
if the institution wants better
discovery of research that's

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:47.160
happening by scholars
at their institutions,

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:49.710
if they want to facilitate
researchers' ease of getting

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:53.370
into the system, then we
connect with single sign

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:56.110
on, if they have an
oauth type of connection,

00:25:56.110 --> 00:25:59.100
so that researchers can log
in with their own university

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:00.240
accounts.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:04.650
And we have a face page
for those universities

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.420
to be able to
track what research

00:26:06.420 --> 00:26:10.650
is being done and made available
on the Open Science Framework.

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:15.150
So that is, like all of our
services, a free service.

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:17.370
And so we have a
variety of institutions

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:20.010
that have signed up
for that and have now

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:23.430
their own interfaces for
engaging with the Open Science

00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:25.830
Framework and
facilitating researchers'

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:29.470
engagement with the service.

00:26:29.470 --> 00:26:33.450
But that sort of leads
to the top layer, which

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:36.450
is the community services
aspect of this, which

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:39.930
is putting these services in
the hands of the communities

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:43.380
themselves to have
their own governance

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:47.010
and standards for how it is
they work with these services.

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:50.550
So I want to give an example
of that with OSF pre-prints.

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:53.820
So we launched this
service earlier this fall,

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:57.660
which is an open pre-print
repository network,

00:26:57.660 --> 00:26:59.810
and that's the address.

00:26:59.810 --> 00:27:05.040
But it draws on a few key pieces
of those underlying layers.

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:09.210
The first is that the search
in the pre-print service

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:12.480
is not search of just
pre-prints on OSF,

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:15.390
but search of pre-prints
across any pre-print provider.

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:18.930
So here are the ones that are
connected so far, so that it's

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:21.840
an aggregator of pre-prints
wherever those pre-prints are

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:22.520
stored.

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:24.972
And so this draws
on the Share service

00:27:24.972 --> 00:27:26.430
that connects all
of these services

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:28.290
together into a single format.

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:31.220
And so search goes on Archive,
Bio Archive, Cogprints,

00:27:31.220 --> 00:27:33.570
[INAUDIBLE] Archive,
PeerJ Sci Archive, RePEc,

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:35.700
Soc Archive, for now.

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:40.100
And then as more things
get added to Share, then

00:27:40.100 --> 00:27:43.590
they're also represented
in that search.

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:45.330
It also has a very
simple workflow

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:48.360
for adding one's own pre-prints
so that you can post pre-prints

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:51.480
very easily to
accelerate communication.

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:55.890
The OSF version of it is for
any discipline, is agnostic just

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:59.950
to facilitate sharing of
pre-prints wherever you are.

00:27:59.950 --> 00:28:03.030
But the key part is
that that interface can

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:05.410
be branded for any community.

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:09.660
So we have open invite for
partnerships of different

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:11.880
groups that want to start
to promote pre-prints

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:15.030
in their particular community,
whether it is disciplinary,

00:28:15.030 --> 00:28:17.740
social sciences,
whether it is topical--

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:20.850
there is a group for open
optimism that is going to have

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:22.410
a pre-print service--

00:28:22.410 --> 00:28:25.080
or whether it is institutional.

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:27.930
And so the Soc Archive
is one branded version

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:29.610
of that pre-print service.

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:33.600
This should release today,
is what I hear from the team.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:37.740
Likewise today should come out
Engineering Archive and Psych

00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:40.620
Archive for psychology.

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:43.500
So all of them have their
own steering committees,

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:46.350
their own standards
for what, content wise,

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:48.690
can be included
in their service.

00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:52.020
And they have minimized
their infrastructure costs

00:28:52.020 --> 00:28:54.540
because of the
shared infrastructure

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:55.710
that supports all of them.

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:57.501
So they don't have to
reinvent any of that.

00:28:57.501 --> 00:29:00.120
They can focus on their
community and engagement

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:01.800
with their community.

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:04.870
So just to illustrate
what pre-print looks like,

00:29:04.870 --> 00:29:09.090
so there's the pre-print as
it's shown in the browser.

00:29:09.090 --> 00:29:11.820
It pulls on the file
rendering tools that

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:13.440
are part of that core service.

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:16.450
It draws on the OSF
projects themselves

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:20.670
and uses all of the contributor
tools that are there,

00:29:20.670 --> 00:29:23.730
has links to DOIs if the article
is published so that you can

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:26.010
represent it there, and
then tags and things

00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:28.500
for simplified discovery.

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:31.270
That model applies
to that overall model

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:33.040
that I described
at the beginning.

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:35.440
We have that core
set of tools that

00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:38.470
enable the creation of a
pre-prints interface on top

00:29:38.470 --> 00:29:40.480
of it that draws on those tools.

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:43.300
And then there are
branded interfaces,

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:46.570
community-based interfaces
using that sort of core

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:48.970
interface to make it
easier for communities

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:53.170
to engage their researchers,
their people in their community

00:29:53.170 --> 00:29:55.310
on those services.

00:29:55.310 --> 00:29:59.420
From our strategic perspective,
the reason to do these layers

00:29:59.420 --> 00:30:02.160
is partly for
efficiency, but also

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:05.810
for allowing experts
to be experts.

00:30:05.810 --> 00:30:08.330
As a psychologist,
I might think I

00:30:08.330 --> 00:30:10.910
want to create a
pre-print service,

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:13.040
but in order to create
a pre-print service,

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:15.440
I have to build all these
tools that aren't really

00:30:15.440 --> 00:30:16.010
my interests.

00:30:16.010 --> 00:30:19.790
I'm interested in scholarly
communication in my community.

00:30:19.790 --> 00:30:23.240
And so by layering these as
independent entities that

00:30:23.240 --> 00:30:26.420
can be built on one another,
we can let technical experts

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:28.370
do the technical
work of how is it

00:30:28.370 --> 00:30:32.000
that you deal with multiple
concurrent interactions moving

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:34.790
to scale for data
storage and transfer?

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:38.952
There's a lot of hard things
that, at scale, the technology

00:30:38.952 --> 00:30:40.910
needs to be able to do,
and do very efficiently

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:42.870
and effectively.

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:45.620
And that's not something
that a psychologist-- me--

00:30:45.620 --> 00:30:46.527
cares about.

00:30:46.527 --> 00:30:48.110
That's something
that they care about,

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:50.980
and so let them be
expert at those tools.

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:53.570
Scholarly communication
experts care about how

00:30:53.570 --> 00:30:54.850
is it that we are interacting?

00:30:54.850 --> 00:30:56.600
How is it that we are
trying to get people

00:30:56.600 --> 00:30:58.670
around this research lifecycle?

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:01.190
Let them have their
expertise in thinking

00:31:01.190 --> 00:31:02.210
about those interfaces.

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:05.570
How is it that scholars should
engage with their research,

00:31:05.570 --> 00:31:07.940
design those interfaces,
and then make those

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:09.770
interfaces available
to the content domain

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:12.590
experts to engage
their communities.

00:31:12.590 --> 00:31:15.230
And so by segmenting them,
we get lots of efficiency

00:31:15.230 --> 00:31:18.320
by cost, but also efficiency
by allowing experts

00:31:18.320 --> 00:31:20.930
to focus on their areas
of expertise and interest

00:31:20.930 --> 00:31:22.430
rather than saying,
oh, well, I have

00:31:22.430 --> 00:31:24.960
to do these things in order
to get the thing that I want.

00:31:27.849 --> 00:31:29.390
[INAUDIBLE] question
and answer part,

00:31:29.390 --> 00:31:31.681
but I just want to make sure
you're clarifying on this.

00:31:31.681 --> 00:31:36.110
So what you're basically doing
is saying [INAUDIBLE] platform

00:31:36.110 --> 00:31:39.228
because you're feeding in
[INAUDIBLE] I want to create

00:31:39.228 --> 00:31:42.342
a pre-print server in this area,
you're basically saying we can

00:31:42.342 --> 00:31:44.010
help you get up and running in--

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:45.510
Tomorrow.

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:49.010
So you send the logo, you
send the phrase that you want.

00:31:49.010 --> 00:31:50.970
So back up to this thing.

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:53.610
You send us this
logo and the name.

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:55.287
You send us this phrase.

00:31:55.287 --> 00:31:57.120
You tell us-- it's not
shown below-- but you

00:31:57.120 --> 00:32:00.210
tell us what are the
relevant disciplines for you.

00:32:00.210 --> 00:32:03.217
And then we set it up,
and then you have it.

00:32:03.217 --> 00:32:04.870
How many have done that so far?

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:07.170
So we have those three
will launch today,

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:09.720
I think, and there
are three more that

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:13.939
are signing the agreement for
us to offer those services.

00:32:13.939 --> 00:32:15.480
And then there are
a number of others

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:17.188
that have expressed
interest, but they're

00:32:17.188 --> 00:32:20.470
organizing their communities.

00:32:20.470 --> 00:32:22.140
So we have that for pre-prints.

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:24.910
But you can instantly, now that
we have all of these tools,

00:32:24.910 --> 00:32:28.760
you can imagine, at this
layer, generalizing that.

00:32:28.760 --> 00:32:31.516
So the next thing that will
be coming out is registries.

00:32:31.516 --> 00:32:32.890
Let's make it so
that anybody can

00:32:32.890 --> 00:32:36.010
create a registry of what's
happening in their research

00:32:36.010 --> 00:32:36.970
domain.

00:32:36.970 --> 00:32:38.800
So it draws on these same tools.

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:41.740
It has very similar
interface as pre-prints,

00:32:41.740 --> 00:32:44.530
but now it has particular
about that registration

00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:46.840
process of documenting what
it is that the research is

00:32:46.840 --> 00:32:48.020
going to be.

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:49.850
And we have partners
with egap and Ridie

00:32:49.850 --> 00:32:52.780
already to move their
registries into this service.

00:32:52.780 --> 00:32:55.360
And we have our own registries
that we already operate

00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:56.800
that will be integrated there.

00:32:56.800 --> 00:32:58.000
But same concept.

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:01.210
Dramatically low on the
barriers so that these groups

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:03.910
can focus on what is it
that's important to register

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:05.140
in developmental economics?

00:33:05.140 --> 00:33:07.660
What do we have to say
about the research process

00:33:07.660 --> 00:33:09.940
that we're doing in these RCTs?

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:13.780
And they answer those questions
of what's the content of it,

00:33:13.780 --> 00:33:16.450
while these services worry
about how is it that it actually

00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:19.740
gets executed and gets done?

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:23.490
So that-- how much
time do I have for--

00:33:23.490 --> 00:33:24.798
seven minutes.

00:33:24.798 --> 00:33:26.790
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:26.790 --> 00:33:30.315
You don't want to open that
up because I really like that.

00:33:33.550 --> 00:33:35.880
So let me talk just
in the last seven

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:37.350
minutes about sort
of the direction

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:38.500
that we're going with this.

00:33:38.500 --> 00:33:42.990
So those services are intended
to provide more access, lower

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:46.770
the barrier to entry to
doing more open things.

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:50.820
The longer term strategy is
to address two challenges,

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:52.530
I think, to openness.

00:33:52.530 --> 00:33:56.650
And that is rooted in the
idolization of publication

00:33:56.650 --> 00:33:58.540
as the thing for advancement.

00:33:58.540 --> 00:34:01.380
So one is to
separate publication

00:34:01.380 --> 00:34:04.290
of scholarly content from
evaluation of that content.

00:34:04.290 --> 00:34:06.570
Right now all of
my incentives are

00:34:06.570 --> 00:34:09.980
get it published, because that's
the mechanism of reward for me.

00:34:09.980 --> 00:34:12.239
And so for researchers,
if we can separate

00:34:12.239 --> 00:34:16.020
the concept of publication
from evaluation,

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:18.659
then what we're
doing is separating

00:34:18.659 --> 00:34:22.020
what is really the basis of
reputation management reward--

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:25.380
evaluation of the quality
of my research-- from what

00:34:25.380 --> 00:34:27.714
is right now just
confounded with it

00:34:27.714 --> 00:34:29.130
but not essential
for it, which is

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:31.800
the publication of that, the
sharing of that in the broader

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:32.300
community.

00:34:32.300 --> 00:34:33.860
And pre-prints
already does that.

00:34:33.860 --> 00:34:36.300
Pre-prints, essentially,
is publishing

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:38.620
before evaluation has occurred.

00:34:38.620 --> 00:34:42.120
Second is that the publication
model, as it currently exists,

00:34:42.120 --> 00:34:45.639
doesn't treat scholarly
content as a public good.

00:34:45.639 --> 00:34:49.830
The basis of most monetization
strategies about publication

00:34:49.830 --> 00:34:52.560
are on controlling
access to that content

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:55.139
rather than making
it openly accessible.

00:34:55.139 --> 00:34:59.760
So you can imagine how this
community of pre-print services

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:01.120
can evolve.

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:04.570
So as more pre-print
services become available,

00:35:04.570 --> 00:35:07.920
then they all develop different
standards, different norms

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:10.260
for what it is that's
content, the kind of content

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:11.670
that they host.

00:35:11.670 --> 00:35:13.110
And as a consequence,
each of them

00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:16.380
develop reputations, not
just for the content domain,

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:19.110
but for their quality standards,
for their evaluation standards

00:35:19.110 --> 00:35:21.630
of what kinds of
research gets in.

00:35:21.630 --> 00:35:23.970
And so what we're building
right now to enhance

00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:26.820
these pre-print
services is a Comments

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:31.410
and Discussion pane so that
anybody in the community

00:35:31.410 --> 00:35:34.410
interact about that
pre-print right

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:38.070
where the pre-print is, aligned
with post-publication peer

00:35:38.070 --> 00:35:38.640
review.

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:40.515
If you consider the
pre-print made available,

00:35:40.515 --> 00:35:43.710
now there can be peer review
discussion, perhaps peer review

00:35:43.710 --> 00:35:45.480
discussion just
before you submit it

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:48.510
to a formal journal for
formal peer review process.

00:35:48.510 --> 00:35:50.970
So that is active
development now,

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:53.130
and then we're also
in the planning stages

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:55.200
for building evaluation
of moderation

00:35:55.200 --> 00:36:01.560
tools for pre-print services to
employ before they include it

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:04.530
in their service.

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:08.460
So Soc Archive, they'll
let any social science in.

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:13.020
I'm going to start a
social science pre-print

00:36:13.020 --> 00:36:15.420
service that's really
focused on methodology

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:16.620
and have a high bar.

00:36:16.620 --> 00:36:18.930
The methodology has to be
really solid before I let it

00:36:18.930 --> 00:36:20.860
into our pre-print service.

00:36:20.860 --> 00:36:25.200
And so I have an evaluation
system where we do peer review,

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:31.070
and we decide, is this worth
including in our service?

00:36:31.070 --> 00:36:34.290
Once those two things are
done, then by and large,

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:35.520
this is publication.

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:37.740
It's the entire
publication system.

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:39.830
And if we add-- it's
too low here to see--

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:42.260
so I'll say some key
features of this,

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:45.530
in order to facilitate openness,
are that the authors retain

00:36:45.530 --> 00:36:46.700
licensing.

00:36:46.700 --> 00:36:49.142
So the author says
it's CC-BY or CC 0.

00:36:49.142 --> 00:36:50.600
They're are not
passing the license

00:36:50.600 --> 00:36:52.820
over to the pre-print service.

00:36:52.820 --> 00:36:55.800
Articles are versioned--
remember version control--

00:36:55.800 --> 00:36:57.140
so there's no finished article.

00:36:57.140 --> 00:36:59.750
I can continue to revise
based on the feedback

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:02.480
that the community gives
about the quality of that work

00:37:02.480 --> 00:37:05.630
rather than right now, the model
of once challenges or things

00:37:05.630 --> 00:37:07.580
to improve in an
article identified,

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:09.455
if I'm motivated to do
it, I write a comment,

00:37:09.455 --> 00:37:11.788
and that comment is published
as a separate thing, which

00:37:11.788 --> 00:37:13.400
is never discovered
when people are

00:37:13.400 --> 00:37:15.650
looking at the original thing.

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:18.920
And it opens up the
opportunity to diversify

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:20.750
evaluation heuristics.

00:37:20.750 --> 00:37:24.690
So how is the information
about the quality of that work

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:25.790
communicated?

00:37:25.790 --> 00:37:29.090
Right now the heuristic
that is most accessible

00:37:29.090 --> 00:37:30.699
is the grade level
of the journal.

00:37:30.699 --> 00:37:32.740
Is that an A journal, is
that an A minus journal,

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:34.010
is that a B journal?

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:36.230
And what I do in my
publication process

00:37:36.230 --> 00:37:39.297
is I submit to them serially
until I get it into one.

00:37:39.297 --> 00:37:40.880
It's an A. No, it's
not an A. A-minus.

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:41.840
No, it's not an A minus.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:42.360
B plus.

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:43.430
No, it's not a B plus.

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:45.890
And so this is a huge waste
of time for everybody.

00:37:45.890 --> 00:37:47.930
So you can imagine a
service that instead

00:37:47.930 --> 00:37:51.200
of assigning a grade
level, it says,

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:52.650
we're going to assign the score.

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:56.290
Submit it to one service
and they say, you got a 75.

00:37:56.290 --> 00:38:00.434
That's a C. And I
say, am I OK with a C?

00:38:00.434 --> 00:38:02.100
At least I got that
information quickly.

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:04.160
It's a C. But I
think it's better,

00:38:04.160 --> 00:38:07.460
and so I respond to the
reviews, I make changes,

00:38:07.460 --> 00:38:10.730
and I resubmit it, thinking
that I can increase my grade.

00:38:10.730 --> 00:38:12.950
And so you can have a much
more efficient process

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:16.800
of evaluation, which we use as
the heuristics for publication

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:20.690
more generally by allowing
diversification innovation

00:38:20.690 --> 00:38:24.320
on how evaluation happens
in an open system.

00:38:24.320 --> 00:38:27.200
The other implication
of authors licensing

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:32.130
is that it breaks the
one-to-one model of one article

00:38:32.130 --> 00:38:34.610
is in one journal.

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:37.750
So if I am holding the
license, then all of these

00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:39.460
are becoming
evaluation services.

00:38:39.460 --> 00:38:41.500
They're not the
publication services.

00:38:41.500 --> 00:38:43.169
So I could submit
to multiple of these

00:38:43.169 --> 00:38:44.710
that have different
reputations, that

00:38:44.710 --> 00:38:46.293
reach different
communities, that have

00:38:46.293 --> 00:38:48.190
different interested users.

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:51.040
Now all of them can provide
their own evaluation,

00:38:51.040 --> 00:38:52.870
and all of those
could be a basis

00:38:52.870 --> 00:38:55.941
of reputation enhancement, of
what the quality of the work

00:38:55.941 --> 00:38:56.900
is.

00:38:56.900 --> 00:38:58.420
So there's a lot
that can be done

00:38:58.420 --> 00:39:01.060
once there is an
open set of services

00:39:01.060 --> 00:39:05.500
for innovation in how scholarly
communication happens.

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:08.270
And so really, if we think
about it as pre-print service,

00:39:08.270 --> 00:39:10.390
it's really now a community
of article services.

00:39:10.390 --> 00:39:14.890
It's pre-print as a term
becomes less relevant.

00:39:14.890 --> 00:39:16.580
So we're close enough so done.

00:39:16.580 --> 00:39:19.810
So I just want to
close with what

00:39:19.810 --> 00:39:22.480
our hope is for a public
goods infrastructure

00:39:22.480 --> 00:39:24.160
like this will do.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:27.860
One is that it ensures that
the content and data are open.

00:39:27.860 --> 00:39:32.710
They're always accessible,
rather than variably accessible

00:39:32.710 --> 00:39:34.810
or not at all accessible.

00:39:34.810 --> 00:39:37.030
Our goal is to
promote monetization

00:39:37.030 --> 00:39:39.970
of commercial services
on providing service,

00:39:39.970 --> 00:39:42.070
not on controlling access.

00:39:42.070 --> 00:39:45.130
So if we can create a
core set of services,

00:39:45.130 --> 00:39:48.550
that services ecosystem, that
any service can connect to,

00:39:48.550 --> 00:39:52.180
but no service has control of
the entire research lifecycle,

00:39:52.180 --> 00:39:55.750
then what the services are
doing is competing for users.

00:39:55.750 --> 00:39:58.900
What additional things can
they provide to the researchers

00:39:58.900 --> 00:40:00.320
to make themselves
more efficient,

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:01.840
to make them more effective?

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:04.480
And that's what should
be the basis of charging,

00:40:04.480 --> 00:40:07.840
rather than once I get you
in to my suite of services,

00:40:07.840 --> 00:40:10.490
then I keep you in
my suite of services.

00:40:10.490 --> 00:40:12.250
It also promotes
interoperability.

00:40:12.250 --> 00:40:15.190
By having a single
ecosystem, it's

00:40:15.190 --> 00:40:16.960
in my interest as
a service provider

00:40:16.960 --> 00:40:19.450
to make sure that my
stuff works and connects

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:21.430
with all of the other services.

00:40:21.430 --> 00:40:24.220
And, as we talked about
with the pre-print example,

00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:26.830
it can massively increase
the efficiency of creating

00:40:26.830 --> 00:40:28.700
and maintaining those services.

00:40:28.700 --> 00:40:31.300
Any group right now that wants
to start a pre-print service

00:40:31.300 --> 00:40:32.695
doesn't need to think
about the technology.

00:40:32.695 --> 00:40:35.050
They just need to think
about how their community can

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:36.684
interact with it.

00:40:36.684 --> 00:40:39.100
And then obviously, by having
public goods infrastructure,

00:40:39.100 --> 00:40:42.610
search discovery and aggregation
can improve dramatically.

00:40:42.610 --> 00:40:45.220
And ultimately we
promote innovation

00:40:45.220 --> 00:40:46.660
and scholarly communication.

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:48.260
Right now it's very
hard to innovate

00:40:48.260 --> 00:40:50.562
in scholarly communication
because the mechanisms

00:40:50.562 --> 00:40:52.270
of scholarly communication
are controlled

00:40:52.270 --> 00:40:55.810
by particular organizations
that are doing very

00:40:55.810 --> 00:40:58.180
well in the system as
it exists, and so there

00:40:58.180 --> 00:41:01.900
isn't a lot of motivation
for innovation in the broader

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:03.610
scope.

00:41:03.610 --> 00:41:06.504
But an open platform facilitates
that more dramatically.

00:41:06.504 --> 00:41:08.920
So I will stop there so that
we have a little bit of time,

00:41:08.920 --> 00:41:11.050
perhaps, for
questions, and you can

00:41:11.050 --> 00:41:13.930
look at pictures of the Center
for Open Science while we talk.

00:41:13.930 --> 00:41:16.075
Thank you.

00:41:16.075 --> 00:41:21.420
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:21.420 --> 00:41:24.300
We do have a few minutes
for questions for Brian.

00:41:24.300 --> 00:41:26.050
There are people at
the front and the back

00:41:26.050 --> 00:41:29.442
of the room with microphones,
so if you have questions,

00:41:29.442 --> 00:41:30.733
please look for the microphone.

00:41:35.170 --> 00:41:37.060
There's a mic right there.

00:41:37.060 --> 00:41:38.470
Hi.

00:41:38.470 --> 00:41:42.130
So fantastic and
inspiring and wonderful.

00:41:42.130 --> 00:41:43.930
Two quick questions.

00:41:43.930 --> 00:41:47.080
So what's the response to
people, though, in terms

00:41:47.080 --> 00:41:49.840
of getting them to
move over to an open--

00:41:49.840 --> 00:41:52.300
to SOC archive or one
of the open pre-print

00:41:52.300 --> 00:41:55.380
archives in terms of maintaining
their reputation scores?

00:41:55.380 --> 00:41:56.880
So in other words,
the big challenge

00:41:56.880 --> 00:42:01.510
is, I de-listed myself from SSRN
10 years ago because of SSRN's

00:42:01.510 --> 00:42:04.240
controlled access policies,
the refusal, in particular,

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:08.200
to allow me to include a
hyperlink in an abstract.

00:42:08.200 --> 00:42:10.450
So I am proud of having
waged that battle.

00:42:10.450 --> 00:42:13.330
But I shot myself in the foot
professionally because I have

00:42:13.330 --> 00:42:14.990
no download scores from SSRN.

00:42:14.990 --> 00:42:17.560
And so I think the question
is, from an incentive

00:42:17.560 --> 00:42:20.260
perspective for
individual users,

00:42:20.260 --> 00:42:22.960
how do you persuade
people to move?

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:24.460
The second quick
question has to do

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:26.920
with integration with or
any relationship with Vivo,

00:42:26.920 --> 00:42:29.710
because you're building
these enormous communities

00:42:29.710 --> 00:42:32.800
and the question of how you
then build up, again, access

00:42:32.800 --> 00:42:35.900
to the reputation of the people.

00:42:35.900 --> 00:42:39.970
How do we connect into and
make more usable the knowledge

00:42:39.970 --> 00:42:42.070
and know how with
these communities that

00:42:42.070 --> 00:42:45.700
may come, in part, from, again,
their articles, but again,

00:42:45.700 --> 00:42:48.100
what's back of those articles
is some fabulous people.

00:42:48.100 --> 00:42:53.590
So I think the open source
reputation systems like Vivo

00:42:53.590 --> 00:42:55.412
might be an interesting--

00:42:55.412 --> 00:42:56.620
that's an interesting player.

00:42:56.620 --> 00:42:59.020
There might be some
other play there to tap

00:42:59.020 --> 00:43:00.891
into the expertise of people.

00:43:00.891 --> 00:43:01.390
Great.

00:43:01.390 --> 00:43:03.040
Thank you for those questions.

00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:07.360
To take the second one first,
there is one Vivo integration

00:43:07.360 --> 00:43:11.200
as those grants that we gave
for integrations to the services

00:43:11.200 --> 00:43:12.880
ecosystem, because
I agree with you.

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:16.510
The kinds of data and material
available about individuals

00:43:16.510 --> 00:43:18.670
in those kinds of
services could be

00:43:18.670 --> 00:43:22.360
really useful to connect to the
actual content of the things

00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:23.950
that they have
contributed, which

00:43:23.950 --> 00:43:25.570
is the real aim with that.

00:43:25.570 --> 00:43:28.070
But that's still in the piloting
phase or development phase.

00:43:28.070 --> 00:43:29.810
So there's nothing
integrated yet.

00:43:29.810 --> 00:43:31.720
On the first question
of persuading people

00:43:31.720 --> 00:43:35.330
to use these services, or
how they use those services,

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:37.715
we are taking a--

00:43:37.715 --> 00:43:39.670
as the technology
provider, which

00:43:39.670 --> 00:43:42.250
is really what we see
as our role in this--

00:43:42.250 --> 00:43:47.500
is a very open-ended
approach of meet researchers

00:43:47.500 --> 00:43:48.740
where they are.

00:43:48.740 --> 00:43:51.010
So for example, using the
open science framework.

00:43:51.010 --> 00:43:53.500
It has open in the title, but
it doesn't require openness

00:43:53.500 --> 00:43:54.340
at all.

00:43:54.340 --> 00:43:58.150
Researchers can use it entirely
privately for their entire life

00:43:58.150 --> 00:43:59.050
if they want.

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:01.420
They never have to make
anything accessible.

00:44:01.420 --> 00:44:04.300
Instead, we try to solve a
problem that they have today

00:44:04.300 --> 00:44:08.470
with those services and then
make it super easy to go open.

00:44:08.470 --> 00:44:11.650
And then, relating closer
to the pre-prints point,

00:44:11.650 --> 00:44:15.890
the goal is, give
researchers tools

00:44:15.890 --> 00:44:17.890
that they can use to be
better at their research

00:44:17.890 --> 00:44:22.120
as they do it now, and provide
access points to transition

00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:24.700
to a more open workflow
across the entire research

00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:28.719
lifecycle, whenever they see it
as in their interest to do so.

00:44:28.719 --> 00:44:30.010
We don't have to persuade them.

00:44:30.010 --> 00:44:30.970
There's other forces.

00:44:30.970 --> 00:44:33.640
There's lots of other
forces that can persuade.

00:44:33.640 --> 00:44:36.610
We just provide the
services to make it easy so

00:44:36.610 --> 00:44:40.150
that they can see, ah, I can
now share this pre-print early.

00:44:40.150 --> 00:44:41.200
I can get downloads.

00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:42.861
And there's these
ratings out there

00:44:42.861 --> 00:44:44.860
that provide some reputation
enhancement for me.

00:44:44.860 --> 00:44:47.360
I see it is in my
interest to do that.

00:44:47.360 --> 00:44:49.360
So that's really the
approach, at least,

00:44:49.360 --> 00:44:51.250
that we have from
our organization,

00:44:51.250 --> 00:44:52.750
but are working
with others that are

00:44:52.750 --> 00:44:55.305
pushing on the incentives
themselves more directly.

00:45:00.370 --> 00:45:03.500
Yes, please.

00:45:03.500 --> 00:45:04.700
Yes, amazing.

00:45:04.700 --> 00:45:05.860
Thank you.

00:45:05.860 --> 00:45:09.920
It will be even more amazing
if the structures you build

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:12.276
persist over the centuries.

00:45:12.276 --> 00:45:14.990
So could you talk a little
bit about how you're funded

00:45:14.990 --> 00:45:16.948
and what the long-term
sustainability of it is?

00:45:16.948 --> 00:45:17.830
Sustainability plans.

00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:18.329
Yeah.

00:45:18.329 --> 00:45:22.340
So our sustainability model is
to work really fast, figuring

00:45:22.340 --> 00:45:23.584
that will solve it, right?

00:45:23.584 --> 00:45:24.083
No.

00:45:26.690 --> 00:45:28.897
So it's a key question.

00:45:28.897 --> 00:45:30.230
So there are a couple of things.

00:45:30.230 --> 00:45:33.200
Our funding right now is
about eight different funders,

00:45:33.200 --> 00:45:33.860
some federal.

00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:37.250
So we have NIH and
NSF and DRPA funding,

00:45:37.250 --> 00:45:39.520
and then a number of private
foundations, so Laura

00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:42.560
and John Arnold Foundation, John
Templeton Foundation, Alfred P.

00:45:42.560 --> 00:45:45.530
Sloan Foundation, for example.

00:45:45.530 --> 00:45:47.330
And we're continuing
to fundraise.

00:45:47.330 --> 00:45:49.610
Our operating budget right
now is about six and 1/2

00:45:49.610 --> 00:45:51.950
million dollars a year.

00:45:51.950 --> 00:45:57.500
The sustainability
model-- so one key thing

00:45:57.500 --> 00:45:59.379
for the design of these
tools is that we're

00:45:59.379 --> 00:46:00.920
trying to make sure
that it shouldn't

00:46:00.920 --> 00:46:03.830
matter if we continue to exist.

00:46:03.830 --> 00:46:09.030
And so all of the stuff
is open and accessible.

00:46:09.030 --> 00:46:14.480
We are creating '90s-style
mirroring tools so that anybody

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:18.000
can create a mirror of the
content that's available

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:20.150
as public access for the OSF.

00:46:20.150 --> 00:46:22.190
We established, right
in our first year,

00:46:22.190 --> 00:46:24.690
a $250,000 preservation fund.

00:46:24.690 --> 00:46:27.050
So if we were to
disappear, that fund

00:46:27.050 --> 00:46:29.960
would support a read-only
version of all of the data

00:46:29.960 --> 00:46:31.640
that's hosted.

00:46:31.640 --> 00:46:34.010
For now, given thew
amount of data we have,

00:46:34.010 --> 00:46:37.850
that's enough for in perpetuity,
for whatever that means.

00:46:37.850 --> 00:46:41.090
But the overall cost model,
there's funding models

00:46:41.090 --> 00:46:45.500
that we are developing for
the transition from right now

00:46:45.500 --> 00:46:48.030
we are moving out of
proof of concept phase

00:46:48.030 --> 00:46:49.820
into moving to scale phase.

00:46:49.820 --> 00:46:53.840
So we anticipate that we will
be largely foundation federally

00:46:53.840 --> 00:46:56.420
funded for the scaling phase.

00:46:56.420 --> 00:46:59.630
But then, if it
works, and it becomes

00:46:59.630 --> 00:47:01.400
shared public goods
infrastructure

00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:03.770
that we all have
a stake in, then

00:47:03.770 --> 00:47:05.990
we can have a
distributed cost model.

00:47:05.990 --> 00:47:08.630
So every group, based
on how much they use,

00:47:08.630 --> 00:47:11.540
their size, their
resources, contributes some

00:47:11.540 --> 00:47:14.510
to a community-governed
infrastructure.

00:47:14.510 --> 00:47:18.050
So that would be the point
at which COS would not

00:47:18.050 --> 00:47:20.900
be the governing body
of these kinds of tools,

00:47:20.900 --> 00:47:23.420
but would hand it over to a
community of stakeholders.

00:47:23.420 --> 00:47:25.520
And if COS continue to
exist, it would just

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:29.771
be like a technical partner to
help maintain those services.

00:47:29.771 --> 00:47:30.765
Yes, please.

00:47:34.741 --> 00:47:38.210
[INAUDIBLE] one of
your institutions?

00:47:38.210 --> 00:47:38.710
No.

00:47:38.710 --> 00:47:41.330
Yale doesn't have an OSF
Institutions account,

00:47:41.330 --> 00:47:44.940
but we could do that really
fast if that's of interest.

00:47:44.940 --> 00:47:48.140
But yeah, there's been
lots of conversations

00:47:48.140 --> 00:47:49.850
and potential
collaborations developing

00:47:49.850 --> 00:47:51.590
across the university here.

00:47:51.590 --> 00:47:54.590
And I'm personally motivated,
given my academic history,

00:47:54.590 --> 00:47:57.130
to make that happen.

00:47:57.130 --> 00:48:04.420
[INAUDIBLE]

00:48:04.420 --> 00:48:05.600
Good question.

00:48:05.600 --> 00:48:07.070
So it depends on the goal.

00:48:07.070 --> 00:48:09.660
So we have collaborations
directly with universities,

00:48:09.660 --> 00:48:13.040
particularly for facilitating
engagement by researchers,

00:48:13.040 --> 00:48:15.640
and then facilitating
discovery of content.

00:48:15.640 --> 00:48:18.272
So institutional repositories
at different universities,

00:48:18.272 --> 00:48:20.480
that's been where most of
the collaboration has been,

00:48:20.480 --> 00:48:25.580
connecting their IRs to Share
so that metadata feat occurs.

00:48:25.580 --> 00:48:29.930
Deeper integrations are if
there are data storage services

00:48:29.930 --> 00:48:31.580
that the university
is providing,

00:48:31.580 --> 00:48:33.770
that can be integrated
with the OSF.

00:48:33.770 --> 00:48:37.700
And then when researchers
from that institution

00:48:37.700 --> 00:48:41.210
log into their account, their
own institutional data storage

00:48:41.210 --> 00:48:45.740
service is right there as part
of each of their projects.

00:48:45.740 --> 00:48:47.600
So there are lots of
deep possibilities

00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:50.000
with universities.

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:53.420
At the disciplinary level, it's
really more about the community

00:48:53.420 --> 00:48:55.160
norms and incentives
for how it is

00:48:55.160 --> 00:48:56.600
that research gets done there.

00:48:56.600 --> 00:48:58.430
So pre-print services,
by and large,

00:48:58.430 --> 00:49:00.800
we will likely
engage with societies

00:49:00.800 --> 00:49:02.630
because that's really
where more of the

00:49:02.630 --> 00:49:05.090
how do we share scholarship
and what kind of scholarship

00:49:05.090 --> 00:49:06.636
gets shared happens.

00:49:06.636 --> 00:49:08.510
We don't tend to pay
attention to scholarship

00:49:08.510 --> 00:49:10.670
done at our institution
because it's

00:49:10.670 --> 00:49:12.650
going across lots of
disciplinary boundaries

00:49:12.650 --> 00:49:14.810
that I'm in my silo.

00:49:14.810 --> 00:49:17.567
I'm just going to keep
thinking in my silo.

00:49:17.567 --> 00:49:18.565
Please.

00:49:18.565 --> 00:49:28.545
[AUDIO OUT]

00:49:28.545 --> 00:49:33.036
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:33.036 --> 00:49:37.064
[INAUDIBLE] I mean, the point is
you don't need the university.

00:49:37.064 --> 00:49:37.980
We'll engage with you.

00:49:37.980 --> 00:49:39.050
