00:52:54.050 --> 00:52:56.170
Can you speak a little
bit more about the--

00:52:56.170 --> 00:52:58.900
in your pre-print service
or in some of the data--

00:52:58.900 --> 00:53:02.650
about the quality
control, because more data

00:53:02.650 --> 00:53:06.160
includes wonderful data,
and also more crap.

00:53:06.160 --> 00:53:11.020
So whatever the virtues and
weaknesses of the peer review

00:53:11.020 --> 00:53:14.830
system are, it is how we have
come to identify the higher

00:53:14.830 --> 00:53:16.000
quality data.

00:53:16.000 --> 00:53:18.760
And particularly in the
biomedical sciences,

00:53:18.760 --> 00:53:21.460
the conduct of the study and
the collection of the data

00:53:21.460 --> 00:53:24.980
is critical to whether we should
continue to use that data set.

00:53:24.980 --> 00:53:28.450
So I'm not sure I understand how
your pre-print service differs

00:53:28.450 --> 00:53:31.360
from peer review
recognized journals.

00:53:31.360 --> 00:53:33.730
Who are those people
doing those reviews,

00:53:33.730 --> 00:53:36.940
and how do we peer
review those reviewers?

00:53:36.940 --> 00:53:38.740
So good questions.

00:53:38.740 --> 00:53:42.430
The quality control
is essentially

00:53:42.430 --> 00:53:45.670
the core function, a filtering
mechanism of the peer review

00:53:45.670 --> 00:53:46.260
process.

00:53:46.260 --> 00:53:47.610
What's worth reading?

00:53:47.610 --> 00:53:51.470
And whatever its flaws,
that has some value.

00:53:51.470 --> 00:53:53.950
So the pre-print services,
the communities themselves

00:53:53.950 --> 00:53:55.450
that launch their
pre-print services

00:53:55.450 --> 00:53:58.450
decide on their own
quality control standards.

00:53:58.450 --> 00:54:01.930
So Archive, for
example, has a standard

00:54:01.930 --> 00:54:05.890
of, is it science and
does a reviewer that

00:54:05.890 --> 00:54:08.510
has some expertise in that
discipline read it and say,

00:54:08.510 --> 00:54:09.970
yep, that's
scholarship that fits

00:54:09.970 --> 00:54:13.150
the broad parameters of what
we are willing to share.

00:54:13.150 --> 00:54:14.450
And it has been very effective.

00:54:14.450 --> 00:54:19.160
That's a longstanding--
it's 400,000 submissions,

00:54:19.160 --> 00:54:20.980
or no, it's less than that.

00:54:20.980 --> 00:54:24.969
But there's, like, 1.2 million
pre-prints on archive now.

00:54:24.969 --> 00:54:26.260
And that's been very effective.

00:54:26.260 --> 00:54:28.510
Bio Archive has its
own set of standards

00:54:28.510 --> 00:54:30.700
for what it is that
it will include

00:54:30.700 --> 00:54:32.290
in its pre-print service.

00:54:32.290 --> 00:54:36.810
So our goal is to facilitate
the creation of many services.

00:54:36.810 --> 00:54:38.950
You'll say, oh, Bio
Archives isn't really good

00:54:38.950 --> 00:54:40.300
enough for what I want to do.

00:54:40.300 --> 00:54:41.920
I'm going start
my own, and we'll

00:54:41.920 --> 00:54:43.402
have a different
set of criteria.

00:54:43.402 --> 00:54:44.860
And then let the
marketplace decide

00:54:44.860 --> 00:54:47.880
whether yours is more worth
looking at than Bio Archive's

00:54:47.880 --> 00:54:49.019
is worth looking at.

00:54:49.019 --> 00:54:50.060
For us it doesn't matter.

00:54:50.060 --> 00:54:52.480
We just want to
facilitate the creation

00:54:52.480 --> 00:54:55.480
of different ways of doing that
evaluation, that filtering.

00:54:55.480 --> 00:54:59.050
And just like how
we absorb our news,

00:54:59.050 --> 00:55:01.445
the services will
provide information

00:55:01.445 --> 00:55:03.070
about what's worth
paying attention to.

00:55:03.070 --> 00:55:05.824
If it's in Huffington Post
versus in New York Times,

00:55:05.824 --> 00:55:07.240
I might have
different assumptions

00:55:07.240 --> 00:55:10.420
about whether that
information is worth reading,

00:55:10.420 --> 00:55:12.910
and the same will be true
of these kinds of services.

00:55:12.910 --> 00:55:17.838
So we won't impose it from
the technical provider.

00:55:17.838 --> 00:55:18.814
Yeah, please.

00:55:23.205 --> 00:55:24.580
First of all, I
just want to say,

00:55:24.580 --> 00:55:26.630
in principle, I applaud
what you're doing

00:55:26.630 --> 00:55:29.620
and I think it is overall
a wonderful thing.

00:55:29.620 --> 00:55:31.530
But I was wondering if you've--

00:55:31.530 --> 00:55:34.460
and this is more of an
existential question.

00:55:34.460 --> 00:55:35.460
And as an example--

00:55:35.460 --> 00:55:37.360
Should I lie down?

00:55:37.360 --> 00:55:39.040
In the aftermath
of the election,

00:55:39.040 --> 00:55:40.414
one of the lobbying
groups that's

00:55:40.414 --> 00:55:42.460
gotten a lot of attention
is a conservative group

00:55:42.460 --> 00:55:44.650
that seeks to cut government
funding for what they consider

00:55:44.650 --> 00:55:45.730
to be wasteful research.

00:55:45.730 --> 00:55:49.060
And they're seeking to align
with animal rights groups

00:55:49.060 --> 00:55:52.810
to say-- right now their target
is to cut dog research done

00:55:52.810 --> 00:55:53.620
by the NIH.

00:55:53.620 --> 00:55:56.200
And they've made very clear
they want to eventually just cut

00:55:56.200 --> 00:55:59.380
funding for all animal
research across the board,

00:55:59.380 --> 00:56:02.650
for any government funded.

00:56:02.650 --> 00:56:05.980
I know that at Yale, the
university cautions us

00:56:05.980 --> 00:56:08.650
about putting any animal
experiment images that

00:56:08.650 --> 00:56:10.150
might be accessible
to the public

00:56:10.150 --> 00:56:12.900
because that could be used
against the university.

00:56:12.900 --> 00:56:15.970
You know, lab members
could get stalked, right?

00:56:15.970 --> 00:56:18.920
And I was wondering,
there's a possibility

00:56:18.920 --> 00:56:21.610
that your tool may actually
stifle the very innovation

00:56:21.610 --> 00:56:23.320
that you seek to propagate.

00:56:23.320 --> 00:56:25.330
And I understand it's
not an easy question

00:56:25.330 --> 00:56:26.710
and there's not answer
on that, but I just

00:56:26.710 --> 00:56:27.970
want to get your
thoughts on that.

00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:28.469
Yeah.

00:56:28.469 --> 00:56:30.310
No, that is a very
important one,

00:56:30.310 --> 00:56:33.640
is that any move to
a cultural change

00:56:33.640 --> 00:56:35.740
will have unintended
consequences that

00:56:35.740 --> 00:56:37.910
are hard to anticipate.

00:56:37.910 --> 00:56:40.210
And so incremental
revolutions are

00:56:40.210 --> 00:56:43.300
good because they allow
corrections along the way.

00:56:43.300 --> 00:56:44.590
You do one step.

00:56:44.590 --> 00:56:45.546
You see what happens.

00:56:45.546 --> 00:56:46.420
You make corrections.

00:56:46.420 --> 00:56:47.920
You do another step.

00:56:47.920 --> 00:56:50.410
And so that, I think,
is really where

00:56:50.410 --> 00:56:52.810
this whole reproducibility
and openness

00:56:52.810 --> 00:56:55.360
movement is going if it
goes effectively, is really

00:56:55.360 --> 00:56:57.700
trying and testing,
and then making sure

00:56:57.700 --> 00:57:01.030
that the kinds of
risk situations

00:57:01.030 --> 00:57:04.330
are addressed effectively,
like what gets shared.

00:57:04.330 --> 00:57:07.300
I have a colleague that
does research on abortion

00:57:07.300 --> 00:57:10.000
and the psychological
effects of abortion.

00:57:10.000 --> 00:57:12.260
And of course the same
sorts of concerns come up.

00:57:12.260 --> 00:57:14.093
How can I possibly share
some of this stuff?

00:57:14.093 --> 00:57:17.740
This is very-- it is so
politicized that there's

00:57:17.740 --> 00:57:19.940
lots of risks for doing that.

00:57:19.940 --> 00:57:21.040
And so we have to have--

00:57:21.040 --> 00:57:24.230
no single imposed solution is
going to be the right solution.

00:57:24.230 --> 00:57:26.710
There'll be lots of occasions
which we say, some of this

00:57:26.710 --> 00:57:28.420
needs to remain closed.

00:57:28.420 --> 00:57:30.450
And it doesn't necessarily
mean entirely closed.

00:57:30.450 --> 00:57:34.420
It may mean closed with
permission to access.

00:57:34.420 --> 00:57:37.330
And so who is it that gets
access to that kind of content

00:57:37.330 --> 00:57:38.680
or that kind of work?

00:57:38.680 --> 00:57:40.780
And so all systems
need to be very--

00:57:40.780 --> 00:57:42.700
any open system,
its top priority

00:57:42.700 --> 00:57:46.189
has to be security because
some stuff can't be opened.

00:57:46.189 --> 00:57:47.980
And when you have an
integrated system that

00:57:47.980 --> 00:57:50.170
has some stuff open
and some stuff not,

00:57:50.170 --> 00:57:53.620
security is priority one because
of exactly the kinds of issues

00:57:53.620 --> 00:57:54.496
that you raise.

00:57:54.496 --> 00:57:54.996
Thanks.

